      "narrative": "The copper lamps gutter as you lean across the scarred table toward Mara Venn. She's been nursing the same cup of dark wine since you sat down, her charcoal coat pulled tight despite the hearth's warmth. Your question hangs in the air between you.\n\nMara's eyes \u2014 sharp, the colour of flint \u2014 hold yours without blinking. She sets her cup down with a soft *clink*.\n\n\"The Ash Gate,\" she says, voice low and dry as paper. \"You want the short answer or the long one?\"\n\nShe doesn't wait for you to choose.\n\n\"Seven days ago, Old Bram \u2014 the ferryman who worked the crossing beyond the Gate \u2014 was found dead at his post. His boat was moored on the far side. The Gate itself was sealed from the inside. No sign of forced entry. No sign of anyone else.\"\n\nShe leans forward, elbows on the table.\n\n\"Toran Vex here \u2014\" she gestures with a thumb at the ink-stained clerk nursing his own cup two tables over, \"\u2014 he maintained the Gate's lock for the Kestrel Guild. Sworn to it. He says the mechanism hasn't been tampered with. The lock wasn't picked, wasn't broken. It was *locked* from the other side by someone who knew exactly what they were doing.\"\n\nHer voice drops further.\n\n\"The Guild sent a courier \u2014 Sable Quill, name's in the ledgers \u2014 through three days ago. She hasn't come back. The Gate stays shut. No one gets in. No one gets out.\"\n\nShe lets that sit.\n\n\"So that's what I know, Kael Windrunner.\" She tilts her head, studying you. \"What I *don't* know is who killed Bram, what happened to Quill, and what's waiting on the other side of that arch that doesn't want company.\"\n\nShe picks up her wine again.\n\n\"You planning on walking through that Gate, or just asking questions to pass the morning?\""
    },
