      "narrative": "The door of the Copper Finch swings shut behind you, cutting off the tavern's warmth. The morning air hits you \u2014 damp and chill, carrying the smell of wet stone and old ash.\n\nYou cross the muddy yard, boots squelching in the churned earth. The path bends around a stand of skeletal birch trees, their bark peeling in pale ribbons. And then you see it.\n\nThe Ash Gate rises alone against the grey sky \u2014 a cracked black arch of volcanic stone, twice the height of a man, its surface glistening with moisture. No wall flanks it. No fence. Just the arch, standing in a patch of bare earth where nothing green grows. Beyond it, the ground drops away into a mist-choked gully where the river runs black and slow.\n\nUp close, the Gate's surface is pitted and scarred. The arch's keystone bears a faded emblem \u2014 a clenched fist gripping a quill \u2014 the mark of the Kestrel Guild. The lock mechanism sits at waist height on the right pillar: a heavy iron box with a single keyhole, its metal dark with age but clean of rust. Fresh tool marks gleam around the screws \u2014 recent maintenance. Toran's work.\n\nThe hasp is thrown. The lock is engaged.\n\nAt the base of the left pillar, slumped against the stone, a travel-stained leather satchel lies abandoned. Its strap is snapped, the buckle tarnished. A few loose papers peek from the unsealed flap, their edges curling in the damp air.\n\nBeyond the Gate, the mist churns. The river gurgles. Nothing moves on the far side."
